\begin{tabular}{|c|p{12cm}|}
\hline
\multicolumn{2}{|c|}{\textbf{Three vulnerable DDoS targets in election contexts}} \\
\hline
\textbf{Step} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
1 &
\textbf{Election results reporting and public information websites:}
Official election-night results portals, county/state election pages, and public dashboards that publish turnout and tallies. These are high-visibility targets and often experience traffic spikes even without an attack. A DDoS can make results unavailable or appear delayed, undermining public confidence and fueling misinformation even if the underlying vote-counting systems remain unaffected. \\
\hline
2 &
\textbf{Voter registration, lookup, and check-in services:}
Online voter registration portals, ``am I registered?'' lookup tools, polling-place locator sites, and electronic pollbook (e-pollbook) back-end services. A DDoS can prevent voters or poll workers from verifying eligibility or locating the correct polling site, causing long lines, provisional ballots, or voter frustration on Election Day. \\
\hline
3 &
\textbf{Election administration communications and supporting infrastructure:}
Email systems, VPN/remote access portals, DNS providers, cloud hosting/CDNs, call centers, and public-facing help lines used by election offices to coordinate logistics and respond to issues. A DDoS against these services can disrupt incident response, slow dissemination of accurate updates, and hinder coordination between state/local offices and vendors during critical periods. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
```

